<p>Each of these buses is proceeding smoothly along the striped street. Yet each seems to be varying its speed dramatically.</p>
<p>When the blue bus encounters a white stripe, it becomes easy to see due to the high contrast, so it appears to move faster. When it encounters a black stripe it becomes harder to see, so its movement seems slower. The reverse is true of the yellow bus. When the stripes are removed, the buses stop staggering.</p>
<p>UCSD psychologist Stuart Anstis first demonstrated the illusion in 2003.</p>
